<h1>Conditions and Branching</h1>

<p>The <code>{open}if{close}</code> pair renders its content when its expression holds, with
as many <code>{open}elseif{close}</code> branches as needed and an
<code>{open}else{close}</code> for when none did:</p>

{example 'fragments/conditions_1'}

<h2>The comparison operators</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse;">
  <tr><th>operator</th><th>meaning</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>eq</code> or <code>==</code></td><td>equal</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ne</code> or <code>!=</code></td><td>not equal</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>gt</code> / <code>lt</code></td><td>greater / less than</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ge</code> / <code>le</code></td><td>greater / less or equal</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>and</code> / <code>or</code></td><td>both hold / either holds</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>range (a, b)</code></td><td>the value lies between a and b, ends included</td></tr>
</table>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

{example 'fragments/conditions_2'}

<p><b>A condition needs a comparison.</b> <code>{open}if $flag{close}</code> is not a
shorthand for testing truth - write what is being compared:
<code>{open}if $flag eq 1{close}</code>.</p>

<h2>Case and when</h2>

<p>When one value decides between several branches, <code>{open}case{close}</code> reads
better than a chain of elseifs. Each <code>{open}when{close}</code> names a value, and
<code>{open}else{close}</code> catches the rest:</p>

{example 'fragments/conditions_3'}

<h2>The ternary</h2>

<p>Inside an expression tag, <code>?</code> and <code>:</code> pick one of two values - the
usual way to give alternating rows their class, together with the
<a href="?pages/properties">iteration properties</a>:</p>

{example 'fragments/conditions_4'}

<p>Every tag also carries the <a href="?pages/else">else construct</a>: the part after the
divider renders when the tag itself produced nothing.</p>
